That gives us a useful distinction: duplication can be computational, not merely redundant.

Two eyes don’t simply give “twice the seeing.” Their slight separation creates disparity, and disparity carries information unavailable to either sensor alone. The delta becomes depth. Two ears similarly turn timing/intensity differences into localization. Bilateral systems can compare simultaneous samples of approximately the same world.

So abstractly:

S_1(x),S_2(x)\rightarrow \Delta=S_1-S_2\rightarrow\text{information}

The pair creates a tiny disagreement on purpose.

Your pupil-as-black-mirror image fits nicely too. It looks like a black surface from outside because we’re looking into an aperture optimized to receive. The interesting inversion is that the eye is phenomenologically our window outward while, viewed from another person, the pupil is a dark opening inward. Same bridge, opposite interpretations depending on which endpoint you’re standing at.

But I wouldn’t make “paired = sensor, singular = processor” a biological law. We have paired kidneys, lungs, gonads, cerebral hemispheres, etc., for different developmental, redundancy, capacity, and specialization reasons. The more portable principle is:

When nature supplies separated channels exposed to a shared event, inspect the delta between the channels.

And now the zipper.

I think you’ve identified something more precise than “time is a zipper.” At each local present there is a many-to-one collapse in action:

\{\text{possible next states}\}\rightarrow\boxed{\text{one realized next state}}

Not necessarily a quantum claim—just a decision/control-system description. Before acting, multiple futures can be represented. After acting, history records one trajectory. Then that realized state becomes the starting condition for the next branching.

So: how to choose?

Perhaps the answer isn’t to know the correct future. That’s impossible in sufficiently uncertain environments. Instead, choose using paired evaluators, just as the sensory systems do.

One evaluator asks:

What happens if I do this?

The other asks:

What happens if I don’t?

Now you’ve manufactured binocular vision for decisions.

\Delta(a)=V(\text{act})-V(\text{don't act})

But we can improve it. Run additional counterfactual “eyes”:

a^*=\arg\max_a
[
\text{expected benefit}
+\text{information gained}
+\text{optionality preserved}
-\text{irreversible downside}
]

That produces a powerful rule under uncertainty:

When you cannot know which future is correct, prefer actions that make the next choice better informed without unnecessarily destroying alternatives.

That’s exploration plus preservation.

And it answers something important about your zipper. The zipper doesn’t have to select the entire future at once. It only closes one tooth.

Then reality supplies the delta.

Possibilities → choose → consequence → compare → update → possibilities′ → choose again.

Which connects directly to the Ouroboros-with-metabolism idea: the consequence isn’t merely history. It becomes nutrient for the next decision. The past therefore sits on the opposite side of the zipper not as dead information but as compressed training data for selecting the next tooth.

There’s an even stranger symmetry:

Two eyes triangulate space.
Memory + prediction triangulate time.

Memory gives one endpoint. Prediction supplies another hypothetical endpoint. The acting self occupies the bridge between them. Reality then tells you how wrong the predicted endpoint was.

That error—the delta—may be one of the most valuable signals the system ever receives.

So perhaps the chooser doesn’t need an oracle.

It needs good anchors, multiple perspectives, cheap reversible probes, preservation of optionality, and ruthless harvesting of prediction error.

You don’t defeat uncertainty before choosing.

You choose in a way that makes uncertainty teach you.